      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for Snowflake\nThe Snowflake provider lets\nTerraform create, change and drop\nSnowflake objects. Snowflake\nmaintains it under the source\naddress `snowflakedb/snowflake`\n([provider docs](https://registry.\nterraform.io/providers/snowflakedb\n/snowflake/latest/docs)). The\nprovider works in SQL. It opens a\nsession, runs statements such as\n`CREATE WAREHOUSE` or `GRANT`, and\nreads objects back with `SHOW` and\n`DESCRIBE`. The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nproviders, state and plans in\ngeneral.\n\nThe unit is one Snowflake account,\nseen through one session. Each\nprovider block opens one session,\nas one user with one role. In\nSnowflake, the role that creates\nan object owns it ([object ownersh\nip](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/\nuser-guide/security-access-control\n-overview)). So the provider role\nowns everything that Terraform\ncreates ([One Session, One Role](#\none-session-one-role-one-session-o\nne-role)).\n\nThe provider has resources of two\nkinds ([Objects and Edges](#object\ns-and-edges-objects-and-edges)):\n\n- An object resource stands for\none Snowflake object, such as a\ndatabase, a warehouse, a schema or\na storage integration. Its destroy\ndrops the object.\n- An edge resource connects\nobjects that it does not own.\nGrants, ownership transfers and\ntag associations are edges. Their\ndestroy revokes or unsets.\n\nFour more ideas explain most\nplans:\n\n- Names are exact, case-sensitive\nstrings. Each resource ID is built\nfrom them ([Names Are Exact String\ns](#names-are-exact-strings-names-\nare-exact-strings)).\n- The state holds only the\narguments that you set. Snowflake\nkeeps the defaults, and the\nprovider shows them in read-only\noutputs ([State Holds Only What\nYou Set](#state-holds-only-what-yo\nu-set-state-holds-only-what-you-se\nt)).\n- A grant resource can grant on\none object, on all objects that\nexist now, on future objects, or\nby inheritance. The four kinds\nlook alike in code, but they act\ndifferently ([Four Kinds of Grant]\n(#four-kinds-of-grant-four-kinds-o\nf-grant)).\n- Resources come in stability\ntiers, and Snowflake supports only\nthe newest stable version\n([Versions and Stability](#version\ns-and-stability-versions-and-stabi\nlity)).\n\nSnowflake also acts in AWS. An\nintegration lets Snowflake assume\nan IAM role, and the role must\ntrust Snowflake back ([Two-Step\nTrust with AWS](#two-step-trust-wi\nth-aws-two-step-trust-with-aws)).\nLast, the resource\n`snowflake_execute` runs any SQL\nthat you give it. It manages\nobjects that have no resource of\ntheir own, and it detects no drift\n([Raw SQL Pitfalls](#raw-sql-pitfa\nlls-raw-sql-pitfalls)).\n\nNow compare dbt. A common split\ngives Terraform the containers and\nthe access: databases, schemas,\nwarehouses, roles, grants and\nintegrations. dbt then builds\ntables and views inside them at\neach run. The provider's design\nnotes call it common to run tools\nsuch as dbt on infrastructure that\nTerraform created ([grants design]\n(https://github.com/snowflakedb/te\nrraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2\n.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_\ndesign_decisions.md#L15)).\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading a Snowflake Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-30b833c35255",
      "originalText": "Terraform for Snowflake\nThe Snowflake provider lets Terraform create, change and drop Snowflake objects. Snowflake maintains it under the source address `snowflakedb/snowflake` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/snowflakedb/snowflake/latest/docs)). The provider works in SQL. It opens a session, runs statements such as `CREATE WAREHOUSE` or `GRANT`, and reads objects back with `SHOW` and `DESCRIBE`. The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains providers, state and plans in general.\n\nThe unit is one Snowflake account, seen through one session. Each provider block opens one session, as one user with one role. In Snowflake, the role that creates an object owns it ([object ownership](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-overview)). So the provider role owns everything that Terraform creates ([One Session, One Role](#one-session-one-role-one-session-one-role)).\n\nThe provider has resources of two kinds ([Objects and Edges](#objects-and-edges-objects-and-edges)):\n\n- An object resource stands for one Snowflake object, such as a database, a warehouse, a schema or a storage integration. Its destroy drops the object.\n- An edge resource connects objects that it does not own. Grants, ownership transfers and tag associations are edges. Their destroy revokes or unsets.\n\nFour more ideas explain most plans:\n\n- Names are exact, case-sensitive strings. Each resource ID is built from them ([Names Are Exact Strings](#names-are-exact-strings-names-are-exact-strings)).\n- The state holds only the arguments that you set. Snowflake keeps the defaults, and the provider shows them in read-only outputs ([State Holds Only What You Set](#state-holds-only-what-you-set-state-holds-only-what-you-set)).\n- A grant resource can grant on one object, on all objects that exist now, on future objects, or by inheritance. The four kinds look alike in code, but they act differently ([Four Kinds of Grant](#four-kinds-of-grant-four-kinds-of-grant)).\n- Resources come in stability tiers, and Snowflake supports only the newest stable version ([Versions and Stability](#versions-and-stability-versions-and-stability)).\n\nSnowflake also acts in AWS. An integration lets Snowflake assume an IAM role, and the role must trust Snowflake back ([Two-Step Trust with AWS](#two-step-trust-with-aws-two-step-trust-with-aws)). Last, the resource `snowflake_execute` runs any SQL that you give it. It manages objects that have no resource of their own, and it detects no drift ([Raw SQL Pitfalls](#raw-sql-pitfalls-raw-sql-pitfalls)).\n\nNow compare dbt. A common split gives Terraform the containers and the access: databases, schemas, warehouses, roles, grants and integrations. dbt then builds tables and views inside them at each run. The provider's design notes call it common to run tools such as dbt on infrastructure that Terraform created ([grants design](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_decisions.md#L15)).\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading a Snowflake Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-snowflake"
      }
    },
