      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "One Session, One Role\nA `provider \"snowflake\"` block holds the\nsession settings: organization, account, user,\nauthenticator, role and warehouse. The `role`\nand `warehouse` arguments are the defaults of\nthe session ([provider configuration](https://\ngithub.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snow\nflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/index.md#L167-L177)).\nEvery statement that Terraform runs uses this\nrole. The role owns each object that it\ncreates, so later grants and drops also need\nit.\n\nSnowflake divides the admin work between\nsystem roles ([access control considerations](\nhttps://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/secur\nity-access-control-considerations)):\n\n- USERADMIN creates and manages users and\nroles.\n- SECURITYADMIN holds the global MANAGE GRANTS\nprivilege. It can grant or revoke any\nprivilege in the account.\n- SYSADMIN creates warehouses, databases and\ndatabase objects. Custom roles should sit\nunder SYSADMIN.\n\nThe same page advises against ACCOUNTADMIN for\nautomated scripts. It also warns that by\ndefault \"not even the ACCOUNTADMIN role can\nmodify or drop objects\" that a custom role\ncreated, unless that role is granted up the\nhierarchy.\n\nTo act as several roles, a configuration\ndeclares several provider blocks with aliases.\nA resource then picks its session with\n`provider = snowflake.<alias>`. The provider\ndocs forbid `USE ROLE` and `USE WAREHOUSE`\ninside SQL, because they make the provider's\nresults inconsistent ([execute warning](https:\n//github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-sn\nowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/resources/execute.md\n#L12)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-7362f01cf16a",
      "originalText": "One Session, One Role\nA `provider \"snowflake\"` block holds the session settings: organization, account, user, authenticator, role and warehouse. The `role` and `warehouse` arguments are the defaults of the session ([provider configuration](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/index.md#L167-L177)). Every statement that Terraform runs uses this role. The role owns each object that it creates, so later grants and drops also need it.\n\nSnowflake divides the admin work between system roles ([access control considerations](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/security-access-control-considerations)):\n\n- USERADMIN creates and manages users and roles.\n- SECURITYADMIN holds the global MANAGE GRANTS privilege. It can grant or revoke any privilege in the account.\n- SYSADMIN creates warehouses, databases and database objects. Custom roles should sit under SYSADMIN.\n\nThe same page advises against ACCOUNTADMIN for automated scripts. It also warns that by default \"not even the ACCOUNTADMIN role can modify or drop objects\" that a custom role created, unless that role is granted up the hierarchy.\n\nTo act as several roles, a configuration declares several provider blocks with aliases. A resource then picks its session with `provider = snowflake.<alias>`. The provider docs forbid `USE ROLE` and `USE WAREHOUSE` inside SQL, because they make the provider's results inconsistent ([execute warning](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/resources/execute.md#L12)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "one-session-one-role"
      }
    },
