      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Four Kinds of Grant\n`snowflake_grant_privileges_to_account_role`\ngrants in one of four ways. Three of them work\nlike this:\n\n| Kind | Code | What it grants | What destroy\ndoes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| One object | `on_account_object`, or\n`on_schema_object` with `object_name` |\nPrivileges on one named object | Revokes them\n|\n| All | `on_schema_object { all { ... } }` |\nPrivileges on every object of a type that\nexists at apply time | Revokes on every\ncurrent object |\n| Future | `on_schema_object { future { ... }\n}` | A rule for objects created later |\nRemoves the rule and keeps the grants it made\n|\n\nThe provider treats a future grant as a\ntrigger ([on_future decision](https://github.c\nom/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/bl\nob/v2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_\ndecisions.md#L85-L91)). It does not track\nwhich objects an `all` grant touched\n([always_apply decision](https://github.com/sn\nowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2\n.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_decis\nions.md#L77-L84)). So configurations pair an\n`all` grant with a `future` grant: the first\ncovers today's objects, and the second covers\ntomorrow's.\n\nThe fourth kind is the inherited grant, which\nthe provider supports since 2.19.0 with an\n`inherited` block. One inherited grant covers\nevery current and future object of a type in a\ncontainer. The provider's migration guide\ncalls the `all` plus `future` pair a common\npattern that an inherited grant replaces\n([inherited grants](https://github.com/snowfla\nkedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0\n/MIGRATION_GUIDE.md#L1986-L1990)). Inherited\ngrants are a preview feature in Snowflake.\nThey allow no exception for a single object: a\n`REVOKE` on one table appears to succeed, and\nthe role keeps the privilege ([inherited grant\ns](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/in\nherited-grants-intro)).\n\nBy default, a grant resource manages only the\nprivileges that it granted. Other grants on\nthe same object stay. The argument\n`strict_privilege_management` makes the\nresource revoke privileges that it did not\ngrant ([design decisions](https://github.com/s\nnowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v\n2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_deci\nsions.md#L67-L75)).\n\nFuture grants in a database need the global\nMANAGE GRANTS privilege. By default, only\nSECURITYADMIN and ACCOUNTADMIN hold it ([GRANT\nprivilege](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-r\neference/sql/grant-privilege)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-67e4e2efd9db",
      "originalText": "Four Kinds of Grant\n`snowflake_grant_privileges_to_account_role` grants in one of four ways. Three of them work like this:\n\n| Kind | Code | What it grants | What destroy does |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| One object | `on_account_object`, or `on_schema_object` with `object_name` | Privileges on one named object | Revokes them |\n| All | `on_schema_object { all { ... } }` | Privileges on every object of a type that exists at apply time | Revokes on every current object |\n| Future | `on_schema_object { future { ... } }` | A rule for objects created later | Removes the rule and keeps the grants it made |\n\nThe provider treats a future grant as a trigger ([on_future decision](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_decisions.md#L85-L91)). It does not track which objects an `all` grant touched ([always_apply decision](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_decisions.md#L77-L84)). So configurations pair an `all` grant with a `future` grant: the first covers today's objects, and the second covers tomorrow's.\n\nThe fourth kind is the inherited grant, which the provider supports since 2.19.0 with an `inherited` block. One inherited grant covers every current and future object of a type in a container. The provider's migration guide calls the `all` plus `future` pair a common pattern that an inherited grant replaces ([inherited grants](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/MIGRATION_GUIDE.md#L1986-L1990)). Inherited grants are a preview feature in Snowflake. They allow no exception for a single object: a `REVOKE` on one table appears to succeed, and the role keeps the privilege ([inherited grants](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/inherited-grants-intro)).\n\nBy default, a grant resource manages only the privileges that it granted. Other grants on the same object stay. The argument `strict_privilege_management` makes the resource revoke privileges that it did not grant ([design decisions](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/docs/guides/grants_redesign_design_decisions.md#L67-L75)).\n\nFuture grants in a database need the global MANAGE GRANTS privilege. By default, only SECURITYADMIN and ACCOUNTADMIN hold it ([GRANT privilege](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/grant-privilege)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "four-kinds-of-grant"
      }
    },
