    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 8220547269,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-d44e27aa66cc",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1321.2,
      "y": 2200.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 612.5,
      "seed": 962303,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Where to Look First\nRead a Snowflake configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers`. The source tells you\nthe namespace, and the version tells you how\nfar the code is from supported behavior. A\ncommitted `.terraform.lock.hcl` tells you the\nexact build.\n2. Each `provider \"snowflake\"` block. Note the\nauthenticator, the role, the warehouse, the\naliases, `preview_features_enabled` and\n`experimental_features_*`.\n3. The resource types in use. Mark the\ndeprecated and preview types, and count the\n`snowflake_execute` blocks.\n4. Each `snowflake_execute`. Check that\n`revert` undoes `execute`. Look for `IF NOT\nEXISTS`, `OR REPLACE` and secrets in the SQL.\n5. The grants. For each one, find its kind,\nits level (database or schema) and the role\nthat owns its target.\n6. The `lifecycle` blocks. Look for\n`prevent_destroy`, `ignore_changes` and\n`replace_triggered_by`.\n7. The integrations and their IAM roles. Find\nthe source of each external ID and IAM user\nARN.\n8. The identifier strings. Check the case and\nany names joined by hand.\n9. The `import`, `moved` and `removed` blocks.\nThey show what Terraform adopted and what it\nrenamed.\n10. The backend and workspaces. Find which\nstate, and so which databases, each run\ntouches.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-d44e27aa66cc",
      "originalText": "Where to Look First\nRead a Snowflake configuration in this order:\n\n1. `required_providers`. The source tells you the namespace, and the version tells you how far the code is from supported behavior. A committed `.terraform.lock.hcl` tells you the exact build.\n2. Each `provider \"snowflake\"` block. Note the authenticator, the role, the warehouse, the aliases, `preview_features_enabled` and `experimental_features_*`.\n3. The resource types in use. Mark the deprecated and preview types, and count the `snowflake_execute` blocks.\n4. Each `snowflake_execute`. Check that `revert` undoes `execute`. Look for `IF NOT EXISTS`, `OR REPLACE` and secrets in the SQL.\n5. The grants. For each one, find its kind, its level (database or schema) and the role that owns its target.\n6. The `lifecycle` blocks. Look for `prevent_destroy`, `ignore_changes` and `replace_triggered_by`.\n7. The integrations and their IAM roles. Find the source of each external ID and IAM user ARN.\n8. The identifier strings. Check the case and any names joined by hand.\n9. The `import`, `moved` and `removed` blocks. They show what Terraform adopted and what it renamed.\n10. The backend and workspaces. Find which state, and so which databases, each run touches.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "snowflake-where-to-look-first"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9425336465,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-7053beadabe1",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1309.2,
      "y": 2460.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC",
      "width": 418.8,
      "height": 220.0,
      "seed": 592648,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
